WEBVTT

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:18.250
The subject we are going to learn is called formal languages and automata theory. It is

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:25.550
also called theory of computation. It is a core course for B. Tech or B. E. in computer

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:31.710
science and engineering in many of the universities. In some universities it is also a course

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:39.450
for B. Tech I. T. In some places in mathematics department also it is a course for M. S.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:51.130
Mathematics. The topics that will be covered in this course are as follows. First we shall

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:59.370
start with grammars. The four types of grammars some examples ambiguity and so on. Next we

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:07.390
consider the finite state automaton which is the simplest machine model. Then we go

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:14.790
on to context free grammars study in detail some of the properties of context free grammars.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:21.930
Next we study about the push down automaton which is the machine model for context free

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:30.130
grammars. Then we go on to the most general type of the machine Turing machines and we

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:35.570
study about the decidability properties and also complexity in Turing machines.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:42.850
We shall cover some of the advanced topics like grammar systems, regulator rewriting,

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:49.690
L systems and so on. And finally, we will end up with two new models of computation the

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:58.060
DNA computing and the membrane computing. The books which could be followed for this

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:04.520
course are as follows. The first book is written by me and Rama. Introduction to

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:10.260
formal languages automata theory and computation published by Pearson education in 2009.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.960
Harpcraft, Mortwani and Wulman introduction to automata theory languages and computation

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:23.240
that is also Pearson education. Peter Lin's and introduction to formal languages and automata.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:32.260
M. Sipser introduction to theory of computation. John Martin introduction to the languages

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:40.680
and theory of computation. These are some of the books there could be many more books which

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.040
you could follow but as a text book you could follow the first one and the second one.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.800
This subject formal languages and automata theory or you can also call it as theory

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:59.580
of computation. It started in the year 1959 with the formal definition given by

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:12.310
Nam Shomsky. He tried to define what is a grammar, what is a mathematical model of a grammar.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:40.400
The motivation was to study parsing in natural languages like English. So, he tried to look at

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:47.920
the parse trees of the natural language sentences and then try to define what is a grammar. So,

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:55.380
let us take some sentences in English and see how they can be parsed. Take a simple sentence first.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:14.300
The man ate the fruit. How can this sentence be parsed? Starting with the sentence this can

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:23.060
